1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They can run, but they rarely get away.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Night or day, in rural and urban settings,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
high-speed chases can be dangerous and terrifying.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
But when equipped with the latest technology and the best training,

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
law enforcement has the upper hand.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Police Pursuit on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
At speeds in excess of 100 miles per hour, a reckless criminal with no regard for the

8
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:53,400
people or property in his path will do anything and go anywhere to avoid being caught.

9
00:00:53,401 --> 00:00:56,700
For law officers, these are worst case,

10
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,800
stress-filled scenarios with unpredictable outcomes

11
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,540
that unfold second by second.

12
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:05,140
They've committed a violation, and they just want to get away,

13
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:07,340
and they have total disregard for their safety

14
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,040
as well as anybody else's.

15
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,560
That's what I found out to be the case.

16
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:14,660
Any time an officer gets in pursuit, there's excitement.

17
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:16,260
I mean, you're chasing this car.

18
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:18,360
You have no idea why it's running.

19
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:20,760
Your heart is in your throat a few times when you go through

20
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:23,160
some of these intersections because it's

21
00:01:23,161 --> 00:01:27,360
because even though you got the siren blaring, you slow down,

22
00:01:27,361 --> 00:01:28,560
people don't pay attention.

23
00:01:28,561 --> 00:01:31,500
They got the windows rolled up and they're listening to the radio.

24
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:32,800
The thing about when you're in a pursuit,

25
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:35,540
I never was afraid I was going to get killed or hurt.

26
00:01:35,541 --> 00:01:38,100
My concern was always what the offender would do,

27
00:01:38,101 --> 00:01:41,340
and I think that's pretty common for a police officer.

28
00:01:41,341 --> 00:01:42,280
You talk to him.

29
00:01:42,281 --> 00:01:46,720
I was scared what he was going to do to somebody innocent.

30
00:01:46,721 --> 00:01:49,860
When they get out there, you've got a 4,000-pound vehicle.

31
00:01:49,861 --> 00:01:53,160
That's a weapon when it goes at a high rate of speed.

32
00:01:53,161 --> 00:01:54,660
And if you're in a pursuit situation,

33
00:01:54,661 --> 00:01:56,120
you're in danger in the life of the officer,

34
00:01:56,121 --> 00:01:58,400
as well as citizens.

35
00:01:58,401 --> 00:02:00,340
But police officers have more and more

36
00:02:00,341 --> 00:02:02,760
technologically sophisticated help.

37
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:04,840
This patrol car is equipped with the latest

38
00:02:04,841 --> 00:02:06,880
in pursuit technology.

39
00:02:06,881 --> 00:02:09,440
Recently, the California Highway Patrol

40
00:02:09,441 --> 00:02:11,880
implemented heads-up display systems

41
00:02:11,881 --> 00:02:14,780
that provide important data on see-through screens

42
00:02:14,781 --> 00:02:17,520
near the windshields of the patrol cars.

43
00:02:17,521 --> 00:02:18,780
This is called the Combiner.

44
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:21,680
It reflects the image from what you see on the screen back

45
00:02:21,681 --> 00:02:28,220
there and it allows us to see the radar, registration or whatever we might need on

46
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:30,390
this screen so we don't have to look down.

47
00:02:30,391 --> 00:02:32,560
We can always keep our eye on the road and

48
00:02:32,561 --> 00:02:35,920
we can also see through the screen so it doesn't inhibit our vision.

49
00:02:35,921 --> 00:02:37,800
Screen radar.

50
00:02:37,801 --> 00:02:44,180
The heads-up display or HUD is also activated by voice commands which during a pursuit enable

51
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:48,080
the driver to keep his hands on the steering wheel as he checks the license plate number

52
00:02:48,081 --> 00:02:50,520
and registration information of the suspect's vehicle.

53
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:52,600
HUD registration.

54
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:55,080
HUD registration.

55
00:02:56,620 --> 00:03:00,600
Registration number 112-6918.

56
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:04,280
Registration number 112-6918.

57
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,740
Registration check.

58
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:09,120
Registration check.

59
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:11,200
HUD next.

60
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:13,580
HUD next.

61
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,240
This system is just one component

62
00:03:16,241 --> 00:03:18,280
in the latest generation of patrol car.

63
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,080
The cockpit is remarkably uncluttered

64
00:03:21,081 --> 00:03:22,360
compared to most police cars.

65
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,400
Lights, sirens, radar, and other equipment functions

66
00:03:26,401 --> 00:03:28,240
can be controlled with one touch

67
00:03:28,241 --> 00:03:30,980
on a small, ergonomically-shaped control pod

68
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:31,960
next to the driver.

69
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:34,620
These high-tech components were designed

70
00:03:34,621 --> 00:03:36,360
and integrated for a specific purpose,

71
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,260
to help police be effective, yet safe,

72
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,620
while driving on patrol and in pursuits.

73
00:03:41,621 --> 00:03:50,840
I think historically, law enforcement from the very beginning has been in pursuit of the suspects.

74
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:55,860
Vehicular pursuits date to the arrival of the automobile at the turn of the 20th century.

75
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:01,460
The earliest motorized fleets were organized in big cities between 1908 and 1911.

76
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:10,620
Police departments formed motorcycle squads because of the increasing difficulty officers on foot, horse and bicycle were having apprehending speeders.

77
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:15,540
Motorcycles of the time resembled bicycles and were relatively simple to maintain,

78
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:20,500
less expensive than automobiles, and more maneuverable in most situations than the cars of the day.

79
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:25,820
Of course, we weren't back there 100 years ago, but it's pretty safe to assume the first time a beat cop

80
00:04:25,821 --> 00:04:28,860
told somebody to stop and they jumped in a car and drove off,

81
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:32,140
everybody stood around scratching their head and said, I think we need cars.

82
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:36,800
Other than the word police painted on the door,

83
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,660
Nothing was added or modified on police vehicles

84
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:41,920
to set them apart from personal automobiles.

85
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:47,760
Not until the 1920s were red lights and sirens installed

86
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:50,580
to alert pedestrians and traffic on crowded streets

87
00:04:50,581 --> 00:04:51,860
that a police car was approaching.

88
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:56,120
First sirens that started were mechanical in nature,

89
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:57,660
and they were activated by the wind.

90
00:04:57,661 --> 00:05:00,680
And the faster you went, the shriller the sound

91
00:05:00,681 --> 00:05:03,140
and the louder the sound of the siren and the higher it would go.

92
00:05:03,141 --> 00:05:10,160
About this time, the first mobile radios for police cars were installed.

93
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:15,560
But they required several yards of wire wrapped across the roof to act as an antenna.

94
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:21,160
You get experimentation with one-way radio broadcasts, but radios are huge.

95
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:29,100
They're tube systems, and the police agencies contracted with a local commercial broadcast radio system.

96
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:31,500
And so you hear country western music,

97
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,600
and then you hear 1855, there's a radio call for you down

98
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:38,140
on Main Street, you need to go there.

99
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:40,640
The danger of gunfire during a pursuit,

100
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:45,280
as dramatized in Hollywood movies, was all too real.

101
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:47,820
Exchanging gunfire became so prevalent,

102
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,360
it spawned the business of adding bulletproof armor

103
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:53,920
to both police and gangsters' cars.

104
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:58,360
Perhaps that's best illustrated by this 1930 V16 Cadillac,

105
00:05:58,361 --> 00:06:00,960
built for notorious gangster Al Capone.

106
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:04,380
With a top speed of 120 miles per hour,

107
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,760
it featured bulletproof glass and quarter-inch armor plate.

108
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:13,200
It also had three-inch portholes for machine guns and rifles to shoot through.

109
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:17,760
City gangsters weren't the only ones with special automobiles.

110
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:21,420
Their country cousins also modified their getaway cars extensively

111
00:06:21,421 --> 00:06:22,860
to carry illegal goods.

112
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:27,040
Moonshiners are people who would make alcohol at home.

113
00:06:27,041 --> 00:06:28,980
They would make it to sell it during prohibition,

114
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:30,720
but they would run it all around,

115
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:32,600
and they would have false gas tanks.

116
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:33,860
They would hide this stuff in,

117
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:35,760
and of course they would zoom around

118
00:06:35,761 --> 00:06:36,820
and try to get away from the police

119
00:06:36,821 --> 00:06:37,680
who would try to catch them.

120
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:39,880
Police scrambled to keep up.

121
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:41,880
They gained the advantage in pursuits

122
00:06:41,881 --> 00:06:44,500
with the addition of advanced radio communications equipment

123
00:06:44,501 --> 00:06:46,220
operated by a dispatcher

124
00:06:46,221 --> 00:06:47,660
who could rally distant officers

125
00:06:47,661 --> 00:06:49,560
to aid in rural or urban pursuits.

126
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:53,280
He would be able to maintain throw a switch

127
00:06:53,281 --> 00:06:55,160
to where he could then broadcast

128
00:06:55,161 --> 00:06:58,060
to our traffic division, our motor officers,

129
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,500
and other patrol divisions that this pursuit was going on.

130
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:06,000
In the 5th precinct, the address 703 Bowery...

131
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,200
In the post-World War II years,

132
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,580
cars became larger, faster, and more reliable.

133
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,820
This was the beginning of the modern police car.

134
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:17,680
Being late 40s, early 50s,

135
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:19,620
we were beginning to see two-way communication

136
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:25,160
between the police headquarters and the radio car.

137
00:07:25,161 --> 00:07:30,100
The new generation of police cars featured brighter lights and louder sirens,

138
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:35,300
again designed as public safety devices to warn motorists and pedestrians the police were approaching.

139
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,780
But some inventions were created to actually stop pursuits.

140
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:46,420
This ingenious bumper grapple was a clever application of a tool usually installed on tractors

141
00:07:46,421 --> 00:07:48,520
and designed to pick up farming items.

142
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:52,020
Once attached to the front bumper of a patrol car,

143
00:07:52,021 --> 00:07:55,560
the grapple could grasp the rear bumper of a car that needed to be stopped,

144
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:59,100
assuming a police car could get close enough to the violator on the run.

145
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,300
But the real push came in the engineering of police cars

146
00:08:03,301 --> 00:08:05,160
that could be driven faster and safer.

147
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:11,880
In the late 60s, the auto manufacturers started to look at

148
00:08:11,881 --> 00:08:16,880
what they could do and started testing cars for pursuit and police work.

149
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:21,000
They were looking at brakes in particular.

150
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:25,000
alternators, suspension, because you were hitting bumps and

151
00:08:25,001 --> 00:08:26,000
things like that.

152
00:08:26,001 --> 00:08:31,000
Police embraced the muscle cars of the 60s and 70s, like the

153
00:08:31,001 --> 00:08:34,000
Chevrolet Impala, because they had powerful engines and were

154
00:08:34,001 --> 00:08:36,000
built for performance and handling.

155
00:08:36,001 --> 00:08:40,000
Since then, automakers have continued to cater to the law

156
00:08:40,001 --> 00:08:44,000
enforcement market by offering police packages for some of their

157
00:08:44,001 --> 00:08:47,000
models, with factory modifications to the engine,

158
00:08:47,001 --> 00:08:49,000
suspension, and electrical systems.

159
00:08:49,001 --> 00:08:54,400
systems. But as the automobile progressed technologically, the critical role of the

160
00:08:54,401 --> 00:08:59,380
driver also needed to be improved. Police departments would soon subject officers to

161
00:08:59,381 --> 00:09:02,840
the rigors of pursuit instruction and advanced driver training programs.

162
00:09:02,841 --> 00:09:09,760
According to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, police pursuits in

163
00:09:09,761 --> 00:09:13,759
the United States result in more than 300 deaths per year.

164
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:17,000
Police Pursuit will return on Modern Marvelous.

165
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:30,060
Now it's a matter of public record that police officers are better drivers than almost any other group whose job requires them to drive.

166
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:34,120
But better isn't good enough. They must be the best.

167
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:45,440
Driver training, especially for police pursuits, was virtually nonexistent until the late 1970s, when films like this started to send the message.

168
00:09:45,441 --> 00:09:51,440
No other driving profession is required to engage in pursuit.

169
00:09:51,441 --> 00:09:52,440
Firemen don't.

170
00:09:52,441 --> 00:09:54,440
Ambulances don't.

171
00:09:54,441 --> 00:09:57,440
Not even auto racers have to speed through cross traffic,

172
00:09:57,441 --> 00:10:00,440
bust intersections, or avoid pedestrians.

173
00:10:00,441 --> 00:10:02,440
Only cops.

174
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,440
Before you were on your own, you learned by air.

175
00:10:07,441 --> 00:10:09,440
And that was the practice.

176
00:10:09,441 --> 00:10:12,440
You didn't really get any driving training for pursuits

177
00:10:12,441 --> 00:10:14,440
when I went through the academy.

178
00:10:14,441 --> 00:10:18,360
It's come a long way since then, and now we're to the point where we have a six-day

179
00:10:18,361 --> 00:10:22,200
program that the recruits have to go through, which is mandated by the state.

180
00:10:22,201 --> 00:10:27,480
We discuss basic driving techniques all the way up to full-fledged pursuits, again, pursuits

181
00:10:27,481 --> 00:10:31,720
being the most dangerous thing we do.

182
00:10:31,721 --> 00:10:35,720
Each student enrolled in these high-performance pursuit training programs starts with the

183
00:10:35,721 --> 00:10:38,280
basics of vehicle operation.

184
00:10:38,281 --> 00:10:43,280
The very first thing that we tell the student is, when you start your shift, check your

185
00:10:43,281 --> 00:10:43,981
car out.

186
00:10:43,982 --> 00:10:46,960
You can be the best driver in the world, but if your car is not ready to go,

187
00:10:46,961 --> 00:10:48,067
you better leave it there.

188
00:10:48,068 --> 00:10:50,580
And we have to make a complete circle check of the vehicle.

189
00:10:50,581 --> 00:10:53,680
You check the tires, you check the brakes, the horn, the windshield wipers, the sirens,

190
00:10:53,681 --> 00:10:54,722
everything in that vehicle.

191
00:10:54,723 --> 00:10:57,000
So once you get through the vehicle check, now you're ready

192
00:10:57,001 --> 00:10:59,680
to go out on the road.

193
00:10:59,681 --> 00:11:02,644
We do a little bit of lecture, beginning with hand positioning.

194
00:11:02,645 --> 00:11:03,820
We teach 9 and 3 now, not

195
00:11:03,821 --> 00:11:11,360
the old 10 and 2, and certainly not the one-handed drive, which most people have resorted to.

196
00:11:11,361 --> 00:11:16,220
So we teach shuffle steering, which means we shuffle from one hand to the other the

197
00:11:16,221 --> 00:11:22,720
steering wheel and we never cross over the 12 o'clock position on the steering wheel.

198
00:11:22,721 --> 00:11:26,420
Driving instructors contend that positioning the hands at 3 o'clock and 9 o'clock on the

199
00:11:26,421 --> 00:11:30,400
wheel is a more stable way to steer the car.

200
00:11:30,401 --> 00:11:35,160
In order to drive safely at high speeds in a pursuit, officers must comprehend the fundamentals

201
00:11:35,161 --> 00:11:38,880
of vehicle dynamics, such as weight transfer.

202
00:11:38,881 --> 00:11:44,560
If you've got a 2,800 pound car, that's almost a ton and a half, and you make an abrupt turn

203
00:11:44,561 --> 00:11:47,240
to the left or right, you're going to make a weight transfer of all that weight.

204
00:11:47,241 --> 00:11:50,660
In other words, if I turn hard to left here, all that weight's going to go to this right

205
00:11:50,661 --> 00:11:51,660
side.

206
00:11:51,661 --> 00:11:57,000
What we train here is do not make that turn while you're braking.

207
00:11:57,001 --> 00:12:00,000
When you brake, the front of the vehicle goes down.

208
00:12:00,001 --> 00:12:02,000
So now you have a combination of factors.

209
00:12:02,001 --> 00:12:06,480
If you turn and brake at the same time, okay, I'm going to turn, I'm going to get on the

210
00:12:06,481 --> 00:12:08,220
brake here and turn.

211
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:10,780
Now I've got weight going to the right front,

212
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:12,720
and I can put the three-fourths of the weight of the vehicle

213
00:12:12,721 --> 00:12:13,720
on that one tire.

214
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:17,460
Transferring this much weight to one corner of the car

215
00:12:17,461 --> 00:12:20,040
can easily exceed the tire's ability to grip the road

216
00:12:20,041 --> 00:12:22,320
and may lead to a loss of control,

217
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:25,460
especially when the road conditions are wet or icy.

218
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:28,900
Most training facilities have a skid pad,

219
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:32,240
an area of pavement that can be deliberately watered down

220
00:12:32,241 --> 00:12:33,540
to produce slick conditions

221
00:12:33,541 --> 00:12:36,460
and allow better training for rain or snow environments.

222
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:40,480
We would start with basic driving, backing skills,

223
00:12:40,481 --> 00:12:42,480
knowing all four corners of the car,

224
00:12:42,481 --> 00:12:45,480
basic accident avoidance techniques.

225
00:12:45,481 --> 00:12:48,480
Then we would bring them up to another level

226
00:12:48,481 --> 00:12:49,480
called emergency response,

227
00:12:49,481 --> 00:12:52,480
which is basically teaching them racing lines,

228
00:12:52,481 --> 00:12:55,480
how to drive the car at high performance levels

229
00:12:55,481 --> 00:12:57,480
using minimal amount of power.

230
00:12:57,481 --> 00:12:59,480
Then we'd take them from that

231
00:12:59,481 --> 00:13:03,480
and move them more into an introduction to pursuit driving,

232
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:04,480
which can be quite a task for someone

233
00:13:04,481 --> 00:13:06,780
It's not a task for someone who's never talked on the radio

234
00:13:06,781 --> 00:13:10,180
while you're trying to tell a direction and no traffic around.

235
00:13:10,181 --> 00:13:13,980
We take that and bump it up to scenario-based training.

236
00:13:13,981 --> 00:13:14,980
And accelerate on.

237
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:18,080
Students participate in a series of high-speed exercises

238
00:13:18,081 --> 00:13:22,880
to chase down one of the instructors who drives a rabbit car.

239
00:13:22,881 --> 00:13:25,080
Cadets also receive instruction on how to approach

240
00:13:25,081 --> 00:13:29,080
railroad crossings in pursuit situations.

241
00:13:29,081 --> 00:13:30,980
The train crossing activates.

242
00:13:30,981 --> 00:13:32,580
Now, many times during a pursuit exercise,

243
00:13:32,581 --> 00:13:39,695
we'll do if we're the person being pursued, we'll stop right about here.

244
00:13:39,696 --> 00:13:40,980
Because a lot

245
00:13:40,981 --> 00:13:44,280
of driving, like anything else, is common sense and using your head. We want to see

246
00:13:44,281 --> 00:13:49,180
if the cadet has got his wits about him and doesn't stop on a railroad track with the

247
00:13:49,181 --> 00:13:50,180
signal going off.

248
00:13:50,181 --> 00:13:56,180
As the curriculum advances, officers experience greater levels of difficulty in handling

249
00:13:56,181 --> 00:14:01,180
to patrol cars and staying calm, focused, and in control.

250
00:14:01,181 --> 00:14:03,920
They also learn to work in teams to mimic the typical

251
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:08,680
coordination of multiple units involved in pursuits.

252
00:14:08,681 --> 00:14:11,000
The instructors push the scenarios and the students

253
00:14:11,001 --> 00:14:13,700
as far as possible without endangering the officers

254
00:14:13,701 --> 00:14:14,401
or the equipment.

255
00:14:17,140 --> 00:14:18,860
But there's another way to provide training

256
00:14:18,861 --> 00:14:21,660
where the situations can seem extremely dangerous,

257
00:14:21,661 --> 00:14:24,020
yet are still safe for the students.

258
00:14:24,021 --> 00:14:26,480
computerized simulators.

259
00:14:26,481 --> 00:14:29,020
Driver training simulators came online for police

260
00:14:29,021 --> 00:14:31,360
during the 1970s and were operated

261
00:14:31,361 --> 00:14:33,660
by the computers of the era.

262
00:14:33,661 --> 00:14:37,060
Some of the first simulators for pursuit driving

263
00:14:37,061 --> 00:14:38,780
were pretty simple.

264
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:42,060
I mean, it was, you get more complex stuff in an arcade

265
00:14:42,061 --> 00:14:43,560
today than you got back then.

266
00:14:43,561 --> 00:14:46,660
But today's simulators are very sophisticated.

267
00:14:46,661 --> 00:14:48,620
They give you a three-dimensional-like view.

268
00:14:48,621 --> 00:14:50,340
It's a surround-type view.

269
00:14:50,341 --> 00:14:52,500
They develop cockpits so that you can actually

270
00:14:52,501 --> 00:14:56,820
sit like you're in the feel of the police car.

271
00:14:56,821 --> 00:14:59,540
Within today's computer-generated environment,

272
00:14:59,541 --> 00:15:01,560
digital pursuits can be custom designed

273
00:15:01,561 --> 00:15:04,020
for specific training scenarios.

274
00:15:04,021 --> 00:15:06,600
Students can hone their skills in risk assessment,

275
00:15:06,601 --> 00:15:09,460
traffic evaluation, and tactical procedures,

276
00:15:09,461 --> 00:15:13,920
as if they were out on the streets of their communities.

277
00:15:13,921 --> 00:15:16,380
But one of the most effective ways to stop a pursuit

278
00:15:16,381 --> 00:15:20,820
can't really be taught by computer, the pit maneuver.

279
00:15:20,821 --> 00:15:24,120
It's a technique as opposed to actual tactics.

280
00:15:24,300 --> 00:15:31,280
And I think it's brought on by the fact that we now know more about the dynamics of the vehicles that not only we're chasing, but the ones we're using.

281
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:35,480
And that is the pit maneuver, or the pursuit intervention technique.

282
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:42,000
The pursuit intervention technique originated in Southern California during the late 1970s.

283
00:15:42,580 --> 00:15:45,580
It came from the bump and run technique used in stock car racing.

284
00:15:45,581 --> 00:15:52,060
The pit maneuver is initiated by the lead patrol car in a pursuit.

285
00:15:54,580 --> 00:15:59,620
That officer gently taps the front bumper of his car against the rear fender of the suspect's car.

286
00:16:01,300 --> 00:16:06,000
This causes the rear wheels of the suspect to lose traction, resulting in a spin-out.

287
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:12,400
The trailing patrol cars carefully position themselves in order to block the suspect from traveling any further.

288
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:19,180
At driving schools, not all training is behind the wheel.

289
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:21,180
Officers also receive instruction on how to use

290
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:24,620
other highly effective methods of halting a pursuit.

291
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:27,020
One of the things we have now is a technology

292
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:29,820
