These whirlybirds gave police a new perspective from which to coordinate the ground

487
00:28:57,341 --> 00:29:00,500
units.

488
00:29:00,501 --> 00:29:01,940
In the early years of the helicopter,

489
00:29:01,941 --> 00:29:04,820
they discovered it was very effective for patrol

490
00:29:04,821 --> 00:29:05,980
and pursuits.

491
00:29:05,981 --> 00:29:09,160
And the department put numbers on the top of the car.

492
00:29:09,161 --> 00:29:11,580
And that way, the air unit could see the number

493
00:29:11,581 --> 00:29:14,180
and tell the unit exactly where to turn or where

494
00:29:14,181 --> 00:29:16,760
to stop or where to go.

495
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:19,000
In the lakefront city of Chicago,

496
00:29:19,001 --> 00:29:21,900
the police equipped their helicopters with pontoons

497
00:29:21,901 --> 00:29:26,240
to take officers where patrol cars couldn't go.

498
00:29:26,241 --> 00:29:28,540
Rural communities quickly discovered that air support

499
00:29:28,541 --> 00:29:30,400
was the best way to keep fleeing suspects

500
00:29:30,401 --> 00:29:32,120
from disappearing into the countryside.

501
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:38,480
Today, helicopters have become integral

502
00:29:38,481 --> 00:29:41,200
to almost all metropolitan law enforcement agencies'

503
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:42,240
pursuit policies.

504
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:44,840
We utilize a helicopter,

505
00:29:45,380 --> 00:29:47,180
and that's actually one of the issues

506
00:29:47,181 --> 00:29:48,440
that I was looking at recently,

507
00:29:48,441 --> 00:29:51,060
whether we should call off the ground units

508
00:29:51,061 --> 00:29:53,900
when the helicopter is engaged or not.

509
00:29:53,901 --> 00:29:59,040
Back when I first came on, there were a few police helicopters,

510
00:29:59,280 --> 00:30:01,680
but they were not always in position to help you out in the pursuit.

511
00:30:02,320 --> 00:30:04,980
Now we have police helicopters all over the place,

512
00:30:05,160 --> 00:30:08,720
and that is a big value to this, especially in an urbanized area,

513
00:30:08,820 --> 00:30:10,320
in terms of trying to apprehend somebody.

514
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:16,020
But just as pursuit training is mandatory for officers and patrol cars,

515
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:18,640
it's also required for helicopter pilots.

516
00:30:20,300 --> 00:30:23,440
The crew in the helicopter, if they're well-trained and well-equipped,

517
00:30:23,441 --> 00:30:27,460
can take an average police pursuit and make it much safer

518
00:30:27,461 --> 00:30:29,520
and make the officers much more effective.

519
00:30:30,240 --> 00:30:33,760
What we do in the helicopter is to keep that bird's-eye perspective,

520
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:35,440
the big picture of the pursuit.

521
00:30:37,860 --> 00:30:42,580
First and foremost, pilots and tactical flight officers, or TFOs,

522
00:30:42,581 --> 00:30:45,300
must be able to operate the helicopter safely.

523
00:30:46,260 --> 00:30:50,560
Aim over. Pass the door. Throttle. Coming hot.

524
00:30:53,440 --> 00:31:05,160
The pre-flight checklist can have as many as 23 points to review

525
00:31:05,161 --> 00:31:07,700
before the crew scrambles to the scene of a pursuit.

526
00:31:10,580 --> 00:31:13,840
This is a pretty standard configuration for a law enforcement helicopter.

527
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:17,900
The pilot on the 206 series aircraft sits in this seat, the right seat,

528
00:31:17,901 --> 00:31:19,900
and that's not always the case in other aircraft.

529
00:31:19,901 --> 00:31:24,900
The TFO in a 206 series aircraft sits in the left seat.

530
00:31:24,901 --> 00:31:29,900
And if you'll notice, all the equipment, the tactical equipment, is arranged around the TFO.

531
00:31:29,901 --> 00:31:37,900
And for instance, off to the left we have the screen, which is used to view the daytime color camera and the infrared camera at night.

532
00:31:37,901 --> 00:31:43,900
And there's also a computerized moving map system we type in with a keyboard to find the location of a specific address.

533
00:31:43,901 --> 00:31:46,900
The computer actually takes us right to the address.

534
00:31:46,901 --> 00:31:50,900
We've got LOJAC and most people are familiar with what LOJAC is.

535
00:31:50,901 --> 00:31:55,400
Our range is much better than the ground units. Moving down the panel we've got

536
00:31:55,401 --> 00:31:59,140
an 800 megahertz system that lets us talk to anybody on the San Diego Police

537
00:31:59,141 --> 00:31:59,841
Department.

538
00:31:59,842 --> 00:32:03,060
The radio right below that looks the same

539
00:32:03,061 --> 00:32:06,400
but it's actually a digital 800 megahertz system that lets us talk to

540
00:32:06,401 --> 00:32:09,980
virtually any other law enforcement agency in the county that are on different

541
00:32:09,981 --> 00:32:10,740
frequencies.

542
00:32:10,741 --> 00:32:14,420
Audio panels for the pilot, copilot, VHF radios

543
00:32:14,421 --> 00:32:18,180
with a backup for the pilot to talk to the air traffic control.

544
00:32:18,580 --> 00:32:21,240
A GPS with a moving map system is below that.

545
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:27,100
A night sun is a 30 million candle power spotlight

546
00:32:27,101 --> 00:32:31,200
that can brighten the city block so officers in the air and on the ground

547
00:32:31,201 --> 00:32:33,140
can see the area as if it were daytime.

548
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,980
But that's just the start of the night flying technologies.

549
00:32:39,680 --> 00:32:41,680
These are our night vision goggles.

550
00:32:41,681 --> 00:32:44,580
This is what every pilot in the unit flies with every night.

551
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:48,980
It's not just an option, it's a policy that when you take off and you're going to fly at night,

552
00:32:49,080 --> 00:32:50,680
you will have your goggles with you.

553
00:32:50,860 --> 00:32:51,960
These are your lifesavers.

554
00:32:52,920 --> 00:32:57,240
Night vision goggles, or NVGs, were first tested during the Vietnam War.

555
00:32:58,120 --> 00:33:04,540
Since then, the scientific advancement of amplifying light has been quite literally night and day.

556
00:33:06,520 --> 00:33:10,440
The hardest thing for a pursuit environment is you've got to have a pilot

557
00:33:10,441 --> 00:33:14,040
who is aware and situationally aware of what's going on around him.

558
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:16,080
If it's pitch black out in front of them,

559
00:33:16,140 --> 00:33:19,460
it's very difficult to see the obstacles and things that are out there.

560
00:33:20,580 --> 00:33:22,820
What we have here is night vision goggles.

561
00:33:22,980 --> 00:33:25,660
The night vision goggles are the latest technology

562
00:33:25,661 --> 00:33:29,960
for law enforcement to use in their pursuit.

563
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:32,360
Just to break down what we're looking at here,

564
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:35,860
this would be a left and right intensifier tube.

565
00:33:36,480 --> 00:33:40,020
This focus ring right here would be your objective focus ring.

566
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:45,940
The focus ring back here is your diopter adjustment or your eyepiece focus ring.

567
00:33:47,060 --> 00:33:51,800
Near IR energy or ambient light goes in and becomes an electrical signal.

568
00:33:52,200 --> 00:33:56,900
Once it hits the phosphor screen, now we have another visible type signal.

569
00:33:57,360 --> 00:34:02,260
And then it goes through a fiber optic bundle, which goes to a small screen in the back of the goggles,

570
00:34:02,560 --> 00:34:05,980
and it becomes an image that now the human eye can see.

571
00:34:05,981 --> 00:34:09,120
And that's what a law enforcement pilot would be using

572
00:34:09,220 --> 00:34:14,220
to go out and try to find the bad guys, so to speak.

573
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:16,220
The result is astonishing visibility

574
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:18,220
on the darkest nights.

575
00:34:18,320 --> 00:34:20,520
Pilots can clearly see the terrain below,

576
00:34:20,620 --> 00:34:23,340
providing a better view of a fleeing suspect

577
00:34:23,440 --> 00:34:27,240
or of a safe place for an emergency landing.

578
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:31,080
Pilots can augment night vision with forward-looking infrared

579
00:34:31,180 --> 00:34:32,580
or FLIR systems.

580
00:34:32,680 --> 00:34:35,040
Most of what the TFO does during the pursuit

581
00:34:35,041 --> 00:34:38,540
is to run, especially at night, is to run the infrared system

582
00:34:38,541 --> 00:34:41,940
because almost all vehicle pursuits end up in foot pursuits.

583
00:34:42,220 --> 00:34:46,080
If you're using the FLIR, you're going to see the person through the bushes.

584
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:49,840
No matter where they go, it's just a tremendous piece of equipment.

585
00:34:51,080 --> 00:34:54,700
FLIR systems are cameras that are sensitive to the infrared light waves

586
00:34:54,701 --> 00:34:55,880
that emit from heat sources.

587
00:34:56,340 --> 00:34:59,380
They convert the thermal radiation into visible light.

588
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:04,580
Usually the pilot wears the night vision goggles to see the area he's flying into.

589
00:35:05,040 --> 00:35:09,140
while the TFO uses the FLIR to concentrate on the suspect being pursued.

590
00:35:09,620 --> 00:35:14,000
They can actually look inside the car and tell us if we have one or more occupants,

591
00:35:14,440 --> 00:35:16,560
whether or not there may be firearms in the vehicle,

592
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:20,100
and things like that that we can't get from a ground-level perspective

593
00:35:20,101 --> 00:35:21,860
when we're behind the car trying to chase it.

594
00:35:22,900 --> 00:35:25,860
But while police pilots have an eye on the action below,

595
00:35:26,240 --> 00:35:29,640
they must also watch out for news helicopters buzzing above them.

596
00:35:31,740 --> 00:35:35,020
Of course, we have to coexist with the media, and I know that.

597
00:35:35,040 --> 00:35:39,280
But many times when you have that type of a situation going down, the media does get

598
00:35:39,281 --> 00:35:44,480
in the way, especially when we call our helicopter unit up and you have helicopters from all

599
00:35:44,481 --> 00:35:45,480
the different stations around.

600
00:35:45,481 --> 00:35:49,740
They do get in the way and that creates a hazard many times.

601
00:35:49,741 --> 00:35:51,900
They keep in contact with each other.

602
00:35:51,901 --> 00:35:56,960
They have a prescribed channel that they go to, especially if one helicopter is joining

603
00:35:56,961 --> 00:35:58,840
the police helicopter.

604
00:35:58,841 --> 00:36:03,140
Our helicopter will make contact with their pilot and let them know, hey, I'm behind you,

605
00:36:03,141 --> 00:36:05,440
I'm to your left, I'm to your right, or whatever,

606
00:36:05,700 --> 00:36:07,540
just to give them a heads up, hey, we're here,

607
00:36:08,180 --> 00:36:09,340
and they acknowledge that.

608
00:36:10,440 --> 00:36:13,020
They need to get their shots, which we completely understand,

609
00:36:13,340 --> 00:36:15,880
but the cameras allow them to get up much higher,

610
00:36:16,240 --> 00:36:19,760
get the shots from a much safer altitude, and do their job.

611
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:23,440
And we have a tremendous working environment with our local media.

612
00:36:23,500 --> 00:36:25,300
We have three news helicopters here in San Diego.

613
00:36:25,301 --> 00:36:27,720
It's not unusual at all to get L.A.-based helicopters

614
00:36:27,721 --> 00:36:29,300
chasing a pursuit down here.

615
00:36:30,580 --> 00:36:32,300
The media's role in pursuit situations

616
00:36:32,301 --> 00:36:35,900
situations is often scrutinized and its purpose questioned.

617
00:36:38,200 --> 00:36:42,900
At first it was something new and I guess later on we started asking ourselves,

618
00:36:42,901 --> 00:36:47,080
why are we doing this? I mean sensationalism, is it for the ratings,

619
00:36:47,081 --> 00:36:51,882
you know, viewership? Even our viewers were calling and going, why?

620
00:36:51,883 --> 00:36:53,960
What purpose does this serve?

621
00:36:53,961 --> 00:36:56,200
So yeah, we started asking ourselves why.

622
00:36:56,201 --> 00:36:59,500
Because they're cool.

623
00:37:00,600 --> 00:37:01,480
It's drama.

624
00:37:01,640 --> 00:37:03,080
You don't know how it's going to end.

625
00:37:03,400 --> 00:37:07,180
It's like a cliffhanger or however long it goes on.

626
00:37:07,460 --> 00:37:09,300
He could just pull over and give up.

627
00:37:09,660 --> 00:37:11,760
It could be some horrendous car crash.

628
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:15,000
It could be any number of things that happen, but it's drama.

629
00:37:15,540 --> 00:37:17,760
And it's entertainment in many respects.

630
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:24,300
But the drama of pursuit isn't limited to cars on the freeways as seen on the evening news.

631
00:37:24,301 --> 00:37:30,020
It can also involve fast boats when a chase takes to the high seas.

632
00:37:34,100 --> 00:37:41,560
A typical turbine-powered police helicopter like the Bell 206 burns around 30 gallons of fuel per hour.

633
00:37:42,900 --> 00:37:45,480
Police Pursuit will return on Modern Marvels.

634
00:37:45,481 --> 00:37:54,420
When it comes to pursuits on the water, nobody has done it longer or better than the U.S.

635
00:37:54,421 --> 00:37:55,420
Coast Guard.

636
00:37:55,421 --> 00:38:00,360
The origin of the Coast Guard started out at the beginning of our country, from the

637
00:38:00,361 --> 00:38:01,360
Revenue Cutter Service.

638
00:38:01,361 --> 00:38:05,760
And the Revenue Cutter Service was actually initiated as a way to gain revenue for the

639
00:38:05,761 --> 00:38:06,760
country.

640
00:38:06,761 --> 00:38:13,400
So the Revenue Cutter Service would stop the merchant ships coming in and collect tariffs

641
00:38:13,401 --> 00:38:16,200
and if they didn't stop, then they would chase them down

642
00:38:16,300 --> 00:38:18,100
and actually stop them and make them pay up.

643
00:38:18,200 --> 00:38:20,500
So essentially the origins of the Coast Guard

644
00:38:20,600 --> 00:38:25,040
are now beginning the pursuit almost 200 years ago.

645
00:38:25,140 --> 00:38:27,520
Two centuries later, smugglers are still

646
00:38:27,620 --> 00:38:29,120
trying to bring their illegal cargo

647
00:38:29,220 --> 00:38:31,180
into the country by boat.

648
00:38:31,280 --> 00:38:33,380
And the Coast Guard still has a specialized fleet

649
00:38:33,480 --> 00:38:35,120
waiting for them.

650
00:38:35,220 --> 00:38:38,260
Boats like this 110-foot island-class cutter,

651
00:38:38,360 --> 00:38:39,900
the Edisto.

652
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:42,460
The ships are an excellent platform for pursuit.

653
00:38:42,461 --> 00:38:45,160
They have excellent seat capabilities.

654
00:38:45,161 --> 00:38:48,460
We have secure communications, which allow us to talk to our commanders.

655
00:38:48,461 --> 00:38:51,460
We also have high speed capabilities.

656
00:38:51,461 --> 00:38:55,460
We have two engines on board that have a total of 6,000 horsepower,

657
00:38:55,461 --> 00:38:58,460
which will enable us to reach speeds well in excess of 30 knots.

658
00:38:58,461 --> 00:39:02,460
So there are very few boats that we can't chase down at that speed.

659
00:39:02,461 --> 00:39:05,460
There are numerous situations where the Coast Guards are involved in pursuits.

660
00:39:05,461 --> 00:39:11,460
And those could be law enforcement cases where we're actually chasing down a drug-laden boat.

661
00:39:11,461 --> 00:39:15,840
A boat that, say, runs the border and has violated the laws

662
00:39:15,841 --> 00:39:18,700
of the United States, the customs laws.

663
00:39:18,701 --> 00:39:20,700
In many ways, chases on the water

664
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:23,000
parallel pursuits on the highway.

665
00:39:23,001 --> 00:39:24,500
It's a lot of adrenaline pumping.

666
00:39:24,501 --> 00:39:27,920
I mean, it's a very high-stress situation.

667
00:39:27,921 --> 00:39:30,580
As a captain of the ship, you kind of have to take in everything

668
00:39:30,581 --> 00:39:31,920
into consideration.

669
00:39:31,921 --> 00:39:34,420
You're trying to do the mission of stopping the boat,

670
00:39:34,421 --> 00:39:35,960
but you also want to make sure that everyone on board the ship

671
00:39:35,961 --> 00:39:36,960
is safe.

672
00:39:36,961 --> 00:39:40,420
Any situation where you have the possibility of someone shooting

673
00:39:40,421 --> 00:39:43,520
shooting at you or going onto a boat

674
00:39:43,521 --> 00:39:45,760
that you have no idea what's on board,

675
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:47,460
but you know that they're doing something illegal.

676
00:39:47,660 --> 00:39:48,500
That could be dangerous.

677
00:39:48,900 --> 00:39:49,600
Watch it!

678
00:39:49,601 --> 00:39:50,301
You got no more time.

679
00:39:52,500 --> 00:39:53,460
Stop your engines!

680
00:39:54,040 --> 00:39:57,880
When a pursuit becomes difficult to manage,

681
00:39:58,260 --> 00:40:01,060
the Coast Guard has the ability to bring out the big guns,

682
00:40:01,540 --> 00:40:02,240
literally.

683
00:40:02,700 --> 00:40:05,200
Behind me, you can see the .50 caliber machine guns

684
00:40:05,201 --> 00:40:07,260
that we use to actually fire warning shots

685
00:40:07,261 --> 00:40:09,740
and disabling fire if necessary to stop boats.

686
00:40:10,420 --> 00:40:14,620
We go through different forms of hailing, signaling, raising flags, flashing lights,

687
00:40:14,621 --> 00:40:18,180
using loud hailers, numerous things, and then the boat just won't stop.

688
00:40:18,400 --> 00:40:20,680
So at that point, we have to ask permission from our commanders,

689
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:23,940
and depending on the situation, they will either authorize us or not

690
00:40:23,941 --> 00:40:27,920
to conduct warning shots and possibly disable fire on the vessel.

691
00:40:31,920 --> 00:40:36,800
This is a 25-millimeter chain gun, and this actually allows us to fire high-explosive rounds

692
00:40:36,801 --> 00:40:39,300
into vessels if we have to stop them,

693
00:40:39,400 --> 00:40:41,480
or at the conclusion of a boarding,

694
00:40:41,580 --> 00:40:43,660
if we need to destroy the vessel and actually get rid of it

695
00:40:43,661 --> 00:40:45,620
so it's safe for other people to come by the area,

696
00:40:45,920 --> 00:40:47,980
then we can actually be able to destroy the vessel with this gun.

697
00:40:50,060 --> 00:40:52,640
Weather permitting, the task of piloting the cutter

698
00:40:52,641 --> 00:40:55,400
during a pursuit takes place high above the main deck.

699
00:40:58,420 --> 00:40:59,660
This is the open bridge.

700
00:40:59,661 --> 00:41:03,740
We have the controls for the helm, the rudder,

701
00:41:03,741 --> 00:41:10,640
secure communications, electronic navigation system, and depth sounders, and also

702
00:41:10,641 --> 00:41:12,522
compasses and gyro compasses.

703
00:41:12,523 --> 00:41:16,740
But when a chase is on, the real nerve center is one level below.

704
00:41:17,960 --> 00:41:20,035
Welcome to the bridge of the Edisto.

705
00:41:20,036 --> 00:41:22,860
And on the bridge here, we have numerous types of

706
00:41:22,861 --> 00:41:26,900
electronic communications, which allow us to keep in touch with our commanders when we're

707
00:41:26,901 --> 00:41:31,360
engaging in their pursuit. We have satellite communications, which are all secure,

708
00:41:31,361 --> 00:41:36,720
and we have UHF, VHF, HF frequency ranges of communications.

709
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:42,600
We also have differential GPS, which is a global positioning system for navigation.

710
00:41:43,240 --> 00:41:48,640
We utilize paper charts and electronic navigation systems via a computer,

711
00:41:49,020 --> 00:41:53,980
which allow us to safely navigate and continue our progress while we're in pursuit of a vessel.

712
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:57,180
This is our primary radar on the ship.

713
00:41:57,340 --> 00:41:59,200
There's an additional radar on that side also.

714
00:41:59,201 --> 00:42:04,080
So this radar allows us actually to track and to continuously monitor vessels.

715
00:42:04,340 --> 00:42:07,620
We are able to see their speed, what course they're going,

716
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:12,920
and allows us to actually input an intercept course on them and track them down.

717
00:42:13,980 --> 00:42:17,900
The island-class cutters aren't the only ships in the fleet that engage in pursuits.

718
00:42:19,400 --> 00:42:22,640
Port security units use several smaller boats for patrol,

719
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:28,160
including this 25-foot Boston whaler-type design, equipped with serious firepower.

720
00:42:29,200 --> 00:42:35,580
This boat is among the fastest in the fleet and rips across the surface at about 46 miles

721
00:42:35,581 --> 00:42:37,120
per hour.

722
00:42:37,121 --> 00:42:40,780
Lightweight and powerful, very few boaters can outrun it.

723
00:42:40,781 --> 00:42:46,180
But this one can go from zero to 50 miles per hour in about three seconds.

724
00:42:46,181 --> 00:42:51,140
But at only 27 feet, these smaller boats are limited to coastal areas or harbors, and they're

725
00:42:51,141 --> 00:42:56,240
not as comfortable in inclement weather as perhaps this 41-foot utility boat.

726
00:42:56,241 --> 00:42:58,840
It's a workhorse that has a 300-mile range

727
00:42:58,841 --> 00:43:02,640
and can patrol for 10 hours nonstop.

728
00:43:02,641 --> 00:43:04,480
The Coast Guard employs pursuit tactics

729
00:43:04,481 --> 00:43:06,820
similar to its landlocked counterparts,

730
00:43:06,821 --> 00:43:08,920
relying heavily on help from above.

731
00:43:08,921 --> 00:43:09,920
Air support's great.

732
00:43:09,921 --> 00:43:11,800
That's one of the greatest things for a pursuit,

733
00:43:11,801 --> 00:43:14,080
is having eyes in the sky.

734
00:43:14,081 --> 00:43:17,600
Aircraft flying around that can actually see what's going on

735
00:43:17,601 --> 00:43:20,260
and can let you know where you need to go.

736
00:43:20,261 --> 00:43:24,140
So often we'll have air support, and that could be a C-130 type

737
00:43:24,141 --> 00:43:25,140
of aircraft.

738
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:29,840
Many of these C-130s are equipped with a Casper system,

739
00:43:29,841 --> 00:43:34,160
a high-powered infrared camera that can observe smugglers from miles away.

740
00:43:36,160 --> 00:43:41,460
More impressive is that live video can be transmitted directly to seafaring or land-based commanders.

741
00:43:43,260 --> 00:43:46,520
They can observe the pursuit as it unfolds in real time.

742
00:43:47,880 --> 00:43:55,520
This is one more example of the technology that makes it nearly impossible for violators to outrun the law and avoid prosecution.

743
00:43:56,240 --> 00:44:01,000
On the seas, in the air, and on land.

744
00:44:02,840 --> 00:44:05,580
With all the advantages law enforcement gains

745
00:44:05,581 --> 00:44:07,980
from these sophisticated technologies and training,

746
00:44:08,780 --> 00:44:11,020
none actually prevents pursuits from starting.

747
00:44:12,180 --> 00:44:15,740
As long as humans retain their innate fight-or-flight instincts,

748
00:44:16,600 --> 00:44:19,680
when cornered, someone will choose to make a run for it.

749
00:44:20,460 --> 00:44:24,660
And for that reason, law enforcement will continue to push technology

750
00:44:24,661 --> 00:44:30,500
to find safer and more effective ways to minimize the risks of police pursuits.

